  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 512 AM EDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nSurface high pressure/upper level ridge in place over much of the \narea today and tonight will keep weather quiet. Expect partly to \nmostly clear skies, light winds, and highs in the 70s, low 80s. \nOvernight lows will be in the 50s. Patchy fog is possible this \nmorning. \n\nSPC Marginal to Slight Risk for Severe Storms Wed... \n\nThe upper level ridge breaks down and shifts eastward Wednesday \nalong with the surface high as an upper level trough enters into the \nupper Great Lakes. This trough will cross through our forecast area \nand into the Lower Great Lakes by Thursday morning. The \ncorresponding surface low pressure crosses northern Ontario/Lake \nSuperior Wednesday, lifting into Quebec by Thursday afternoon. This \nbrings a cold front across our CWA Wednesday afternoon into late \nWednesday night/Thu AM. There could be a few showers/non-severe \nstorms during the late morning/early afternoon hours near Lake MI \nand in our MI counties before we see better forcing arrive with the \nfront. Once that occurs, models develop a convective line of storms \n(possibly initially discrete) that drift roughly east-southeastward \nthrough late evening. The exact timing and point of initiation is \nstill slightly uncertain-some start the cells over Lake MI and drift \nthem eastward along I 80-90 before filling in further southwestward \nwith time, others develop a broader line initially and eventually \nweaken on the north end, pushing the greatest threat further \nsouthwest. In some cases, it almost appears the lake breeze of Lake \nMI enhances the development, which would trigger storms further east \nof the lakeshore counties. However, most of the guidance has the \nbest severe potential developing between 4 pm-12 am EDT. Forecast \nwise, have broad 40-70 percent pops following this general trend as \na first start. Given the earlier onset time to coincide with peak \nheating, confidence is slightly higher in severe weather occurring \nthan previous forecasts. Models are also slightly higher on mid-\nlevel lapse rates (6.5C/km)...though they still aren't really that \ngreat. Still, much of the guidance has about 1500-2500 J/kg of mixed \nlayer CAPE by the afternoon (some suggest higher further southwest). \n0-6 km bulk shear is slightly higher than previous runs suggested as \nwell, on the order of 25-40 kts. This is enough for supercells to \ndevelop (especially models indicating the higher end of that range). \nWith any supercell development, we have all hazards on the table, \nincluding tornadoes. 0-1 km shear values (20 kts) and SRH values \n(around 100 or more), in combination with low LCL's certainly \nsupport that. These parameters were also part of the reason for \nincreased confidence in severe weather occurring (Medium \nconfidence). \n\nMostly dry outside of a low chance (20 percent) for showers and \nstorms along and south of US 24, mainly east of I 69 on Thursday. \nNorthwest winds behind the front will kick up Lake MI waves to \naround 2-3 feet, which is a moderate swim risk. Currents are \nexpected. Monitor the beach forecast for more details. \n\nSurface high pressure and weak ridging builds in Friday into Sunday, \nallowing for mostly dry conditions. Highs will be in the upper 70s, \nlow to mid 80s.",
